### Software and analytical environment

The clustering analysis was conducted in Python using pandas and NumPy for data manipulation and numerical processing. Matplotlib and Seaborn were used to support exploratory and statistical visualisation. The machine-learning procedures were implemented using scikit-learn. StandardScaler was used to standardise the participant-level features, K-Means was used to identify participant groupings, silhouette analysis was used to assess cluster separation, and Principal Component Analysis was used to produce a two-dimensional visual representation of the clustering solution. A fixed random seed of 42 was specified to improve the reproducibility of the analysis.

### Loading the participant-level dataset

The participant-level feature dataset generated during the feature engineering stage was imported into Python using the `read_csv()` function from the pandas library. Each row represented an individual participant, while the columns contained aggregated behavioural, engagement, emotional, and learning-related measures derived from all storytelling sessions. An initial inspection of the dataset was performed using the `head()` function to verify that the data had been imported correctly and that the variables were available for subsequent clustering analysis.

### Dataset exploration

An initial exploratory assessment of the participant-level dataset was conducted prior to clustering. The structure of the dataset was examined by inspecting its dimensions, variable names, and data types. In addition, the number of non-missing observations for each variable was reviewed to identify any incomplete data that required preprocessing before clustering. This exploratory stage ensured that the dataset was suitable for subsequent unsupervised machine learning analyses.

### Missing data treatment

The participant-level dataset contained a small number of missing observations in the variables *Mean_response_time_seconds* and *Mean_success_percentage*. As the dataset consisted of only 32 participants, removing incomplete cases would have unnecessarily reduced the sample size. Instead, missing values were imputed using the median of each respective variable. Median imputation was selected because it is robust to extreme values and is appropriate for continuous variables such as response times. Following imputation, the dataset was re-examined to confirm that no missing values remained prior to feature scaling and clustering.

### Selection of clustering variables

Prior to clustering, the participant identifier was excluded from the feature matrix because it serves only as a unique identifier and does not represent a behavioural or learning characteristic. Including identifier variables in distance-based clustering algorithms such as K-Means can introduce artificial similarities or differences between observations. Consequently, clustering was performed using the 15 participant-level behavioural, engagement, emotional, learning, and performance features derived during the feature engineering stage.

### Feature standardisation

Prior to clustering, the participant-level features were standardised using the `StandardScaler` implementation from scikit-learn. Standardisation transformed each variable to have an approximate mean of zero and a standard deviation of one. This preprocessing step was necessary because K-Means clustering is based on Euclidean distance and is sensitive to differences in variable scale. Standardising the features ensured that no individual variable exerted disproportionate influence on the clustering solution due solely to its numerical range.


### Removal of non-informative features

During preprocessing, the variable *Number_of_sessions* was removed from the clustering feature set because all participants completed the same number of storytelling sessions. As the variable exhibited zero variance across the dataset, it contained no information that could contribute to distinguishing participants. Removing constant features is consistent with good machine-learning practice, as such variables do not influence distance calculations or improve the quality of clustering solutions.

### Determining the optimal number of clusters: Elbow Method

The optimal number of clusters was initially investigated using the Elbow Method. K-Means clustering models were fitted for values of *k* ranging from 2 to 10, and the within-cluster sum of squares (inertia) was calculated for each solution. Inertia measures the compactness of clusters by quantifying the sum of squared distances between observations and their assigned cluster centroids. The resulting plot was examined to identify the point at which further increases in the number of clusters produced only marginal reductions in inertia, indicating a suitable balance between model complexity and cluster compactness.

The Elbow Method indicated a substantial reduction in inertia as the number of clusters increased from 2 to 5. Beyond five clusters, the rate of improvement became noticeably smaller, suggesting diminishing returns from adding additional clusters. Consequently, values around five clusters were considered suitable candidates for further evaluation using Silhouette Analysis.

The Silhouette Analysis indicated that the highest silhouette score was obtained when the data were partitioned into two clusters, suggesting that this solution produced the greatest separation between participant groups. However, the Elbow Method indicated that a solution of approximately five clusters also provided a suitable balance between model complexity and cluster compactness. As the two evaluation methods produced different recommendations, both statistical evidence and the interpretability of the resulting participant profiles were considered before selecting the final clustering solution.

## Selection of the final clustering solution

The Elbow Method and Silhouette Analysis produced different recommendations regarding the optimal number of clusters. Consequently, K-Means models with both candidate values of k were fitted and compared. The final clustering solution was selected by considering cluster compactness, cluster separation, cluster size, and the interpretability of the resulting participant profiles in the context of personalised storytelling interventions for autistic children.

## Clustering Analysis

An unsupervised clustering analysis was conducted to identify naturally occurring participant profiles based on responses to the personalised storytelling intervention. Clustering was performed at the participant level using the feature-engineered dataset produced during the preceding stage of the analysis. The dataset contained 32 participants and a series of aggregated measures representing engagement, emotional connection, verbal participation, attention, enjoyment, story understanding, learning application, confidence, generalisation, story recall, reflection, real-life connection, response time, and success percentage.

K-Means clustering was selected as the primary clustering algorithm. This method partitions observations into a predefined number of clusters by iteratively assigning observations to the nearest cluster centroid and updating the centroids to minimise within-cluster variation. The approach was considered suitable for the present analysis because the objective was to explore whether participants demonstrated distinguishable multivariate response patterns across the behavioural, engagement, learning, and outcome-related features derived from the storytelling sessions.

## Data preparation for clustering

Prior to clustering, the participant-level dataset was examined for its dimensions, variable types, and missing observations. The dataset consisted of 32 participant records. Missing values were identified in two variables: Mean_response_time_seconds, which contained five missing observations, and Mean_success_percentage, which contained one missing observation. These missing values were imputed before clustering to retain the complete participant sample rather than removing participants with incomplete observations.

The participant identifier was excluded from the clustering features because it functions solely as an identifier and does not represent a meaningful behavioural or intervention-related characteristic. Number_of_sessions was also excluded from the clustering analysis because it showed no meaningful variation across participants and therefore could not contribute to distinguishing participant profiles. Consequently, 14 participant-level variables were retained as clustering features: mean engagement, emotional connection, verbal participation, attention, enjoyment, story understanding, learning application, confidence, generalisation, story recall, reflection, real-life connection, response time, and success percentage.

## Feature standardisation

The selected clustering variables were standardised prior to applying K-Means. Standardisation was necessary because the features were measured on different numerical scales. For example, several engagement and learning measures were represented using relatively small numerical ranges, whereas response time was measured in seconds and success was expressed as a percentage. Without scaling, variables with larger numerical ranges could disproportionately influence the Euclidean distances used by K-Means.

Standardisation transformed the selected features so that they were expressed on a comparable scale, with values representing their position relative to the feature mean and standard deviation. The resulting standardised features were subsequently used for all K-Means model fitting and cluster evaluation procedures.

## Determination of the number of clusters

The appropriate number of clusters was investigated rather than predetermined. Candidate K-Means solutions ranging from two to ten clusters (k=2 to k=10) were evaluated using both the Elbow Method and Silhouette Analysis. A fixed random state was used to support reproducibility, and multiple centroid initialisations (n_init=10) were employed to reduce sensitivity to a single random initialisation.

The Elbow Method evaluated within-cluster sum of squares, represented by K-Means inertia, across the candidate values of k. As the number of clusters increases, inertia naturally decreases because observations can be assigned to increasingly specialised groups. Therefore, the analysis considered the point at which further increases in the number of clusters produced diminishing improvements in within-cluster compactness. Visual inspection of the elbow curve indicated that a solution of approximately five clusters was a plausible candidate.

Silhouette Analysis was additionally employed because the Elbow Method alone does not directly evaluate separation between clusters. The silhouette coefficient considers both the similarity of an observation to observations within its assigned cluster and its separation from observations in neighbouring clusters. Higher silhouette values indicate more cohesive and clearly separated clusters.

The highest silhouette coefficient was obtained for the two-cluster solution (k=2), with a score of 0.545. In comparison, the silhouette coefficients for three, four, and five clusters were 0.242, 0.242, and 0.234, respectively. The substantially higher coefficient for k=2 provided evidence that the two-cluster solution produced considerably clearer separation of participant profiles than the more complex alternatives.

## Comparison of candidate clustering solutions

Because the Elbow Method and Silhouette Analysis did not produce identical recommendations, cluster selection was not based solely on a single evaluation criterion. The two-cluster solution suggested by the Silhouette Analysis and the approximately five-cluster solution suggested by visual examination of the Elbow Method were fitted separately and examined as candidate models. This allowed statistical clustering performance to be considered alongside cluster size and substantive interpretability.

The two-cluster solution divided the 32 participants into groups containing 25 and 7 participants, respectively. In contrast, the five-cluster solution produced clusters containing 11, 6, 6, 8, and 1 participant. The presence of a single-participant cluster in the five-cluster solution was investigated rather than automatically treated as an error or used as grounds for excluding the participant.

Inspection showed that the participant assigned to the singleton cluster exhibited markedly low standardised values across numerous clustering variables. This suggested that the five-cluster model was using one cluster to represent an unusually low multivariate response profile. The participant was retained in the analysis because an unusual response pattern does not, by itself, provide sufficient methodological justification for exclusion and may represent genuine heterogeneity within the participant sample.

Considering the substantially higher silhouette coefficient, the absence of a singleton cluster, and the emerging interpretability of the participant profiles, the two-cluster solution was provisionally preferred over the five-cluster solution. The final interpretation of the selected clusters was subsequently based on cluster-level feature profiles and visual examination of the participant structure.

## Cluster profiling

Following model selection, cluster profiling was undertaken to determine the characteristics differentiating the participant groups. Mean values of the 14 clustering variables were calculated separately for each cluster. This enabled the mathematically derived clusters to be interpreted in relation to participants' responses to the storytelling intervention.

Cluster interpretation was conducted cautiously. Cluster numbers were treated as arbitrary computational labels rather than ordinal categories; for example, membership in Cluster 1 does not inherently represent a better or worse outcome than membership in Cluster 0. Similarly, the resulting profiles were interpreted specifically in relation to observed responses to the storytelling intervention and were not considered indicators of autism severity, general ability, or broader clinical characteristics.

## Dimensionality reduction and visualisation

Principal Component Analysis (PCA) was planned as a dimensionality-reduction technique to support visual examination of the final clustering solution. Because the clustering analysis contained 14 features, the complete participant structure could not be directly represented in a conventional two-dimensional plot. PCA was therefore used to transform the standardised feature space into a smaller number of orthogonal components, allowing participants and their cluster memberships to be visualised in two dimensions.

Importantly, PCA was used for visualisation and interpretation rather than as the primary basis for K-Means clustering. The clustering algorithm itself was fitted using the full set of selected standardised features so that information contained across the original clustering variables was retained during model estimation.

## Reproducibility and methodological considerations

Several measures were incorporated to improve the reproducibility and robustness of the clustering procedure. The same standardised feature matrix was used when evaluating competing values of k, a fixed random state was applied to K-Means, and multiple centroid initialisations were performed. Cluster selection was based on complementary quantitative criteria rather than relying exclusively on visual inspection of a single metric. Candidate solutions were additionally assessed in terms of cluster sizes and substantive interpretability.

The relatively small sample size (n=32) was considered when interpreting the clustering results. The clusters should therefore be regarded as exploratory participant profiles within the present dataset rather than definitive or generalisable subtypes of autistic children. The clustering analysis was intended to identify patterns that could support subsequent interpretation of heterogeneous responses to personalised storytelling interventions, rather than to establish clinical classifications.

