Methodology — Participant Clustering
Cluster analysis

An unsupervised clustering analysis was conducted to identify groups of participants exhibiting similar patterns across the engagement, emotional, behavioural, and learning-related measures. The analysis was performed at the participant level, with each participant represented by their mean scores across the available sessions. The participant-level dataset contained 32 participants.

Participant ID was excluded from the clustering features because it served only as an identifier and did not represent a meaningful behavioural or learning characteristic. The number of sessions was also excluded because it showed no variation across participants and therefore provided no discriminatory information for clustering. Following these exclusions, 14 features were retained for the cluster analysis: engagement, emotional connection, verbal participation, attention, enjoyment, story understanding, learning application, confidence, generalisation, story recall, reflection, real-life connection, response time, and success percentage.

Prior to clustering, missing values were handled using median imputation. This was required for response time and success percentage, for which five and one participant-level values were missing, respectively. Median imputation was used to retain the full participant sample while reducing the influence that extreme values could have on the imputed values.

Because the clustering variables were measured on different numerical scales, the 14 features were standardised using StandardScaler. Standardisation transformed the variables onto a common scale before clustering, preventing variables with larger numerical ranges, such as response time or success percentage, from disproportionately influencing the distance calculations used by K-Means.

Selection of the number of clusters

K-Means clustering was evaluated for solutions ranging from two to ten clusters (k=2 to k=10). A fixed random state and 10 centroid initialisations (n_init=10) were used to improve reproducibility and reduce sensitivity to the initial placement of cluster centroids.

The number of clusters was evaluated using both the within-cluster sum of squares (inertia) and the silhouette coefficient. Inertia was examined using the elbow method to assess how increasing the number of clusters reduced within-cluster variation. The silhouette coefficient was additionally calculated to assess the balance between within-cluster cohesion and separation from other clusters, with higher values indicating better-defined cluster structures.

The evaluation did not rely solely on a single numerical criterion. Candidate solutions were also examined in relation to cluster size and substantive interpretability. In particular, the two- and five-cluster solutions were inspected in greater detail. The five-cluster solution produced cluster sizes of 11, 6, 6, 8 and 1 participants, meaning that one cluster consisted of a single participant. Examination of this participant showed an unusually low profile across many of the clustering measures. However, the participant was retained because the observed values represented an existing participant profile rather than being treated automatically as an erroneous observation.

The final two-cluster solution was selected because it produced the highest silhouette coefficient among the evaluated solutions and provided a more parsimonious and interpretable participant grouping. The final K-Means model was therefore fitted using k=2, and each participant was assigned to one of the two clusters.

Cluster profiling

Following selection of the final model, the clusters were profiled using the mean values of each clustering feature. Standardised cluster profiles were also calculated to allow direct comparison across features originally measured on different scales.

Differences between the mean standardised feature scores of the two clusters were calculated descriptively to identify the variables on which the cluster profiles showed the greatest separation. These differences were treated as descriptive characteristics of the clusters rather than inferential effect sizes, as the same variables had been used to construct the clusters.

PCA visualisation

Principal Component Analysis (PCA) was used as a dimensionality-reduction technique to visualise the final cluster solution. The 14 standardised clustering variables were projected onto two principal components, allowing participants to be represented in a two-dimensional scatter plot according to their K-Means cluster membership.

PCA was used only for visualisation; clustering was conducted using the complete set of 14 standardised features rather than the two PCA components. The proportion of variance explained by each principal component was calculated to assess how much of the original multivariate variation was represented in the two-dimensional visualisation.

Results — Participant Clustering
Selection of the final cluster solution

K-Means solutions containing between two and ten clusters were evaluated. The two-cluster solution produced the highest silhouette coefficient (0.545). Silhouette coefficients were substantially lower for the alternative solutions, including 0.242 for three clusters, 0.242 for four clusters and 0.234 for five clusters. The scores for solutions containing six to ten clusters remained below approximately 0.22.

Although the five-cluster solution was examined as an alternative candidate, its cluster sizes were highly uneven (11, 6, 6, 8 and 1 participants). The single-participant cluster corresponded to Participant 133, who exhibited particularly low values across several engagement and learning-related measures. The participant was retained in the dataset, but the formation of a singleton cluster reduced the usefulness of the five-cluster solution as a general participant segmentation.

Consequently, the two-cluster solution was retained as the final model. It divided the 32 participants into Cluster 0 (n = 25; 78.1%) and Cluster 1 (n = 7; 21.9%).

Characteristics of the two clusters

The cluster profiles revealed a consistent distinction between the two participant groups. Cluster 0 demonstrated higher mean values than Cluster 1 across almost all engagement, emotional and learning-related measures.

For example, mean engagement was 3.21 in Cluster 0 compared with 1.64 in Cluster 1, while mean emotional connection was 2.96 and 1.40, respectively. Mean story understanding was also higher in Cluster 0 (3.26) than Cluster 1 (1.65).

Differences were particularly pronounced for several learning-related measures. Mean confidence was 2.57 for Cluster 0 compared with 0.78 for Cluster 1, while generalisation was 2.27 compared with 0.44. Similarly, mean reflection was 2.38 in Cluster 0 and 0.35 in Cluster 1, and real-life connection was 2.28 and 0.49, respectively.

The clusters also differed in overall success percentage. Cluster 0 achieved a mean success percentage of 74.37%, compared with 44.58% for Cluster 1.

Response time showed a different pattern. Participants in Cluster 0 had a mean response time of approximately 66.42 seconds, whereas Cluster 1 had a mean response time of approximately 74.96 seconds. However, subsequent comparison of the standardised cluster profiles indicated that response time showed substantially less separation between the clusters than the other measures.

Standardised cluster profiles

Examination of the standardised cluster profiles reinforced the distinction between the two participant groups. Cluster 0 showed positive mean standardised scores across almost all engagement and learning measures, whereas Cluster 1 showed consistently negative scores.

The largest descriptive differences between the standardised cluster means were observed for generalisation (2.14), reflection (2.13), real-life connection (2.09), story recall (2.08) and confidence (2.01). Emotional connection, engagement, verbal participation, learning application and story understanding also showed substantial differences between the two profiles.

In contrast, the standardised mean difference for response time was only −0.19, indicating comparatively little separation between the clusters on this measure. Thus, the cluster distinction appeared to reflect a broad pattern across engagement, emotional connection and learning-related outcomes rather than primarily differences in response speed.

PCA visualisation

The PCA supported the visual interpretation of the final cluster structure. The first principal component accounted for 76.84% of the total variance, while the second accounted for 7.76%. Together, the two components explained approximately 84.60% of the variance in the 14 standardised clustering features.

The PCA scatter plot showed that the two K-Means clusters were primarily separated along the first principal component. Cluster 0 participants were predominantly positioned toward the higher end of PC1, whereas Cluster 1 participants occupied the lower end. Participant 133 was positioned particularly far along the negative direction of PC1 and belonged to Cluster 1, consistent with the participant's comparatively low values across several of the original measures.

Overall, the PCA visualisation was consistent with the two-cluster solution identified through K-Means and the silhouette analysis, while also illustrating heterogeneity within the smaller cluster.

